      <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
      <p>This proposal recommends a federally supported pilot program to establish a self-sustaining rehabilitation city on a U.S.-controlled, uninhabited island or remote federal installation. The pilot targets incarcerated individuals judged to be a continuing public-safety risk who are unlikely to succeed if released directly into mainstream society. This approach is fiscally responsible and enhances public safety by securely secluding high-risk individuals while providing them marketable trade skills and restorative programming.</p>

      <h2>Core Objectives</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reduce Recidivism:</strong> Create a structured, purpose-driven community with proven vocational and therapeutic programs.</li>
        <li><strong>Lower Taxpayer Burden:</strong> Build self-sustaining infrastructure fueled by resident labor to offset long-term costs.</li>
        <li><strong>Secure Seclusion:</strong> Provide a humane alternative for those who cannot safely reenter mainstream society, ensuring maximum distance from the general population.</li>
        <li><strong>Restorative Justice:</strong> Create opportunities for victim-offender mediation and meaningful community contribution.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Legislative Provisions</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Authorization:</strong> Pilot program on federally-owned uninhabited islands or suitable remote locations.</li>
        <li><strong>Selection Criteria:</strong> Prioritize public safety and rehabilitation potential for high-risk cohorts.</li>
        <li><strong>Infrastructure Standards:</strong> Mandated housing, renewable energy, food production, and vocational spaces.</li>
        <li><strong>Governance Model:</strong> Trained correctional officers, mental health professionals, and civilian oversight.</li>
        <li><strong>Reporting:</strong> Mandatory reporting to Congress on recidivism, cost, and operational outcomes.</li>
        <li><strong>Earned Release:</strong> Conditions based on demonstrated responsibility and community contribution.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="grid-2">
        <div>
          <h3>Cost Modeling (Pilot)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Capital Investment:</strong> $150–300M (Construction)</li>
            <li><strong>Annual OpEx:</strong> $20–50M</li>
            <li><strong>Projected Savings:</strong> $15–30M/year from labor/recidivism</li>
            <li><strong>10-Year Net Savings:</strong> $50–150M per site</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Quick Facts</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Target Cohort:</strong> 500–1,500 residents</li>
            <li><strong>Security:</strong> Secure natural seclusion</li>
            <li><strong>Revenue:</strong> Vocational exports & energy surplus</li>
            <li><strong>Partners:</strong> DOJ, BOP, DHS, Non-Profits</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Implementation Roadmap</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Year 1:</strong> Legislative authorization, site selection, and environmental review.</li>
        <li><strong>Year 2:</strong> Infrastructure construction, staffing, and specialized training.</li>
        <li><strong>Year 3:</strong> First cohort deployment and continuous monitoring.</li>
        <li><strong>Year 4–5:</strong> Program scaling and refinement based on pilot outcomes.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Metrics for Success</h2>
      <p>Success will be measured through rigorous data analysis, including:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Recidivism rates compared to matched cohorts in traditional prisons.</li>
        <li>Cost-per-resident over 5–10 years vs. status quo.</li>
        <li>Vocational certification and post-program employment rates.</li>
        <li>Independent human-rights audits and oversight compliance scores.</li>
      </ul>

